Patch 01/10 (readw_poll_sleep_timeout) is a small, mechanical addition
following the existing readl_poll_sleep_timeout pattern in
include/linux/iopoll.h - looks correct and low risk on its own.

I see Tom Rini already left several inline "Re:" review comments further
into this thread (at least on patches 01, 03, 04, and 06/10 going by the
Message-IDs referencing this series) - has this iopoll patch itself (01/10)
received any specific feedback, or is it considered mechanical/uncontested
given patches 03+ reuse it (e.g. disk: efi: GPT entries buffer, nvmxip SPL
support)?

Given this is a 10-patch series touching core readw/readl polling, a new
RISC-V SoC's full enablement (reset driver, MMC, RAM, SPI, device trees,
and board support), could you clarify in a reply whether patch 01/10 is
intended to be picked up independently of the rest of the series (since
it's a generic core change useful beyond MT2824), or should it only land
together with the full series?
